@meta
id: 11-04
title: Giving the repeat a new count
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: a regex find-and-replace, or eight manual selections
open: worker.log

@brief
Eight lines of worker log. Every line is prefixed with noise you do not
want: a date, a time, a level. Halfway down the file the worker started
tagging its output as well, so the last four lines carry a fourth token.

Strip the prefix off all eight lines so that each one begins with the
subsystem word — `order`, `payment`, `email`.

Three tokens on the first four lines, four on the last four. You do not
need a second command for that, and you do not need to press the same one
twice.

@keys
| `d3W` | delete three space-separated WORDs |
| `.` | repeat the last change, with the same count |
| `4.` | repeat it with a new count of four |

@hints
`W` counts space-separated chunks, so `2024-03-11` is one of them, not
five. Delete three of them at once on line one.
--
A bare `.` repeats the count you originally gave. Give `.` a count of its
own and that count replaces the old one for good.
--
`d3W`, then `j.` three times, then `j4.` — and from there a bare `.` is
deleting four WORDs, so the rest of the file is `j.` again.

@solution
    d3W   j.  j.  j.   j4.   j.  j.  j.

The count is part of what `.` remembers, which is why `j.` keeps taking
exactly three WORDs. When you type `4.` you are not saying "do it four
times" — you are saying "do that change again, but with four". The new
count then sticks, so every `.` after it also takes four.

That distinction catches people out. `3.` after a `dd` does not delete
three lines three times; it deletes three lines, once. The count in front
of `.` replaces the count you used originally rather than multiplying it.

Being able to re-aim the repeat like this is what keeps `.` useful on
files that are nearly, but not exactly, uniform. You do not have to
abandon the repeat the moment one line is different.

@start worker.log
2024-03-11 08:14:02 INFO order ORD-1182 created
2024-03-11 08:14:03 INFO payment authorised 4800 for ORD-1182
2024-03-11 08:14:03 WARN payment retry 1 of 3
2024-03-11 08:14:04 INFO email receipt queued for CUS-4410
2024-03-11 08:15:01 INFO [worker-3] order ORD-1183 created
2024-03-11 08:15:02 WARN [worker-3] payment retry 1 of 3
2024-03-11 08:15:09 INFO [worker-3] refund issued for ORD-1180
2024-03-11 08:15:12 INFO [worker-3] email receipt queued for CUS-4411

@expected worker.log
order ORD-1182 created
payment authorised 4800 for ORD-1182
payment retry 1 of 3
email receipt queued for CUS-4410
order ORD-1183 created
payment retry 1 of 3
refund issued for ORD-1180
email receipt queued for CUS-4411
